# Target C-II — the dense phase-pure cusp ladder can have growing depth

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** dense fixed cusp-chain phase/bracket theorem and its global sliding-window budgets  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_growing_cusp_dense_ladder_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The dense cusp-chain theorem was stated for every fixed depth `R`. Its proof also permits an explicit growing depth. The only extra bookkeeping is that a length-`R_D` window containing the unique diameter-routing hub must be discarded; there are at most `R_D` such windows, which is negligible when `R_D=o(D)`.

In particular, taking

\[
R_D=\lfloor D^{1/3}\rfloor,
\qquad
\sigma_D=D^{-1/3},
\qquad
\mu=0.01,
\tag{GC.1}
\]

produces `R_D+1 -> infinity` consecutive nonhub selected O--O rows with one common transverse train family `P_D` satisfying

\[
\boxed{|P_D|>(0.6536-o(1))D,}
\tag{GC.2}
\]

and every row-column crossing in the whole growing rectangle has defect

\[
\boxed{
0<\delta_{jb}
\le(1{,}109{,}000+o(1))D^{-1/3}.
}
\tag{GC.3}

The same common column order contains two common circumcenter flanks, simultaneously on every one of the `R_D+1` rows, each of density

\[
\boxed{
0.6536-{1.02\over\sqrt3}-o(1)
=0.0647027254\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{GC.4}

Thus the dense common-ladder geometry is not only a fixed-depth compactness statement: it persists through a polynomially growing sequence of consecutive strips.

---

## 1. Sliding-window averaging remains valid for `R_D=o(D)`

Use the notation of the dense fixed-chain theorem. The selected consecutive high-`t` row block has

\[
m_D>cD-o(D),
\qquad c=0.0000226.
\tag{GC.5}

For each internal interface let

\[
J_i^{\rm full}=N_i+L_i+DB_i+F_i+F_{i+1},
\]

where `F_j` is the full angular-defect sum of row `j`. The global budgets give

\[
\sum_iJ_i^{\rm full}\le K_{\rm glob}D+o(D),
\tag{GC.6}

with the already certified strict inequality

\[
{K_{\rm glob}\over c}<1{,}108{,}000<H,
\qquad H:=1{,}109{,}000.
\tag{GC.7}

Let `R_D` be any positive integer sequence satisfying

\[
R_D\to\infty,
\qquad
R_D=o(D).
\tag{GC.8}

Consider every length-`R_D` window of consecutive internal interfaces in the full row block. Before excluding the hub, the number of such windows is

\[
m_D-O(R_D)=cD-o(D).
\tag{GC.9}

A fixed interface, in particular the unique routing-hub interface, belongs to at most `R_D` length-`R_D` windows. Hence deleting every window containing the hub removes only

\[
O(R_D)=o(D)
\]

windows. The number of admissible hub-free windows is still

\[
cD-o(D).
\tag{GC.10}

Every interface belongs to at most `R_D` admissible windows, so by nonnegativity

\[
\sum_W\sum_{i\in W}J_i^{\rm full}
\le R_D\sum_iJ_i^{\rm full}
\le R_D(K_{\rm glob}D+o(D)).
\tag{GC.11}

Divide by the `cD-o(D)` admissible windows. Since the strict margin in (GC.7) is fixed, for all sufficiently large `D` some hub-free window satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{i=0}^{R_D-1}J_i^{\rm full}<H R_D.
}
\tag{GC.12}

The global `o(D)` error becomes `o(R_D)` after this averaging, so no uniform fixed-`R` asymptotic assumption is being smuggled into the growing-depth step.

---

## 2. The whole growing block shares almost the full crossing family

Let the rows in the selected window be

\[
\tau_0,\ldots,\tau_{R_D}.
\]

Every row has

\[
|\Gamma_j|>r_0D-o(D),
\qquad r_0=0.6536.
\tag{GC.13}

Adjacent terminal topology gives

\[
|\Gamma_j\triangle\Gamma_{j+1}|\le L_j.
\]

The `L_j` part of (GC.12) gives

\[
\sum_{j=0}^{R_D-1}L_j<HR_D.
\]

Starting with `Gamma_0` and passing successively through the rows, at most `L_j` trains can be lost from the running intersection at step `j`. Hence

\[
\left|\bigcap_{j=0}^{R_D}\Gamma_j\right|
\ge |\Gamma_0|-\sum_{j=0}^{R_D-1}L_j
>r_0D-HR_D-o(D).
\tag{GC.14}

Since `R_D=o(D)`,

\[
\boxed{
\left|\bigcap_{j=0}^{R_D}\Gamma_j\right|
>(r_0-o(1))D.
}
\tag{GC.15}

Any two trains in this intersection both cross `tau_0`; triangle-freeness of the complete-train crossing graph makes the common column family internally noncrossing. The standard pseudoline order lemma therefore gives one common column order on all `R_D+1` rows.

---

## 3. Vanishing column deletion gives uniform phase purity through growing depth

The full row-defect part of (GC.12) gives

\[
\sum_{j=0}^{R_D}F_j<HR_D.
\tag{GC.16}

For a common column `b`, let

\[
\Delta_b:=\sum_{j=0}^{R_D}\delta_{jb}.
\]

Then

\[
\sum_b\Delta_b<HR_D.
\tag{GC.17}

Choose any sequence `sigma_D>0` such that

\[
\sigma_D\to0,
\qquad
{R_D\over\sigma_DD}\to0.
\tag{GC.18}

Delete columns with

\[
\Delta_b>{HR_D\over\sigma_DD}.
\tag{GC.19}

There are fewer than `sigma_DD=o(D)` such columns. Call the surviving common family `P_D`. From (GC.15),

\[
\boxed{|P_D|>(r_0-o(1))D.}
\tag{GC.20}

For every retained row-column crossing,

\[
\boxed{
0<\delta_{jb}
\le {HR_D\over\sigma_DD}=o(1),
}
\tag{GC.21}

uniformly over all `0<=j<=R_D` and all `b in P_D`.

This is one common column family through the entire growing block, not separate local ladders.

---

## 4. Concrete balanced choice `R_D=D^(1/3)`

Take (GC.1). Then

\[
{R_D\over\sigma_DD}
=D^{-1/3}+o(D^{-1/3}),
\]

so (GC.21) becomes (GC.3).

The interface depth grows as `D^(1/3)`, the relative column deletion is `D^-1/3`, and the uniform angular defect is also `O(D^-1/3)`.

More generally, if

\[
R_D=D^a,
\qquad
\sigma_D=D^{-b}
\]

with

\[
0<a<1,
\qquad
0<b<1-a,
\]

then the common-column loss is `O(D^{1-b})=o(D)` and the uniform defect is

\[
O(D^{a+b-1})=o(1).
\tag{GC.22}

---

## 5. Circumcenter flanks survive through the entire growing block

Fix `mu=.01`. The exact train-step metric and source circumdisk capacity apply row by row to the same ordered family `P_D`. After the `o(D)` column deletion, each row has more than

\[
\left(
 r_0-{1.02\over\sqrt3}-o(1)
\right)D
\]

retained crossings below `-.01D`, and the same number above `+.01D`.

Since the column order is common on all `R_D+1` rows, the same initial and final column blocks work simultaneously on every row. Therefore there are

\[
P_D^-,P_D^+\subset P_D
\]

with

\[
\boxed{
|P_D^-|,|P_D^+|>
(0.0647027254\ldots-o(1))D
}
\tag{GC.23}

such that every crossing center in `P_D^-` lies below the `-.01D` row-normal level and every crossing center in `P_D^+` lies above `+.01D`, simultaneously for all rows in the growing block.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The fixed-chain theorem is therefore not intrinsically finite-depth. The current high-`t` survivor contains a common ordered train rectangle with

```text
D^(1/3)+1 consecutive rows;
(0.6536-o(1))D common columns;
uniform O(D^(-1/3)) angular defect on every cell;
(0.0647027-o(1))D common columns on each side of a +-0.01D circumcenter corridor on every row.
```

Other sublinear depth/error tradeoffs are available from (GC.22).

This does not close C-II. It removes the possibility that the dense common-ladder geometry is only a bounded-depth phenomenon. A remaining closure can now use a genuinely growing near-equilateral strip with one common transverse shore and common metric bracketing, rather than only a fixed-depth diagonal statement.

**Disposition:** upgraded the dense fixed cusp-chain scaffold to explicit growing depth `D^(1/3)` with near-full common columns, uniform `O(D^-1/3)` phase defect, and common circumcenter flanks. C-II and Level I remain open.